course: dictionary
lesson: 15233
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word shanty is a good example. It can mean a small, rough hut, a song sailors used to sing, or a poor area of homes at the edge of a city.
explain: «The sailors sang a shanty as they pulled on the heavy ropes.» The words sailors, pulled and ropes are the clues. Sailors sang together to keep time while they worked, so a shanty here is a work song, not a hut.
explain: «The family lived in a shanty made of old boards and rusty tin.» Now the clues are boards, tin and lived in. A house made of scrap is a hut, so a shanty here is a small, rough shack.
explain: Always check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence. If «hut» makes sense and «song» does not, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A word can have more than one meaning, so always check which one fits.
fact: «Shanty» means a small, roughly built hut or shack made from scrap materials.
fact: A «sea shanty» is a song sailors sang together while working, to keep time when pulling ropes.
fact: «Shanty town» means an area of very poor homes made of scrap, found at the edge of a city.
exercise: «The sailors sang a shanty as they pulled on the heavy ropes.» What is a shanty here? | *A work song sung by sailors to keep time | A small hut made of scrap | A type of strong rope | A big ship with tall sails
exercise: «The family lived in a shanty made of old boards and rusty tin.» What is a shanty here? | *A small, rough hut made from scrap | A kind of boat | A busy market in the city centre | A song sung on a ship
exercise: «Shanty towns grew at the edge of the city, where many families had no money for proper homes.» What does shanty town mean? | *An area of very poor homes made of scrap | A rich area with big houses and gardens | A town where sailors sing every night | A town by the sea full of fishing boats
test: «The crew sang a shanty while they hauled the nets, and everyone pulled together.» What does shanty mean here? | A shop that sells rope | *A sailors' work song | A rough hut | A poor area at the edge of a city
test: «Ruth built a shanty in the garden from old wood, but it had no proper door.» What does shanty mean here? | A song sung while working | *A small, rough hut | A busy harbour | A shanty town
exam: «The shanty was damp and cold, with holes in the tin roof.» Which clue tells you shanty means a building, not a song? | Pulled on the ropes | Sang together to keep time | *Holes in the tin roof | The crew sang
